nt","donor_padj":0.725582,"donor_log2fc":0.143531,"donor_lfcse":0.129789,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.00782137},{"cell_type":"In","gene_symbol":"PSAP","gene_symbol_current":"PSAP","gene_id":"ENSG00000197746","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":true,"nucleus_direction":"lower in AD","nucleus_padj":1.52492e-65,"nucleus_log2fc":-0.115277,"nucleus_score":-17.2261,"pct_ad":0.768072,"pct_control":0.790396,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.967435,"donor_log2fc":-0.0163427,"donor_lfcse":0.0946608,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":-0.000829379},{"cell_type":"Mic","gene_symbol":"PSAP","gene_symbol_current":"PSAP","gene_id":"ENSG00000197746","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"higher in AD","nucleus_padj":0.636337,"nucleus_log2fc":0.00806037,"nucleus_score":0.604986,"pct_ad":0.770009,"pct_control":0.772235,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.966544,"donor_log2fc":0.0275026,"donor_lfcse":0.116215,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.00378383},{"cell_type":"OPC","gene_symbol":"PSAP","gene_symbol_current":"PSAP","gene_id":"ENSG00000197746","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"higher in AD","nucleus_padj":0.426216,"nucleus_log2fc":0.0341454,"nucleus_score":1.09353,"pct_ad":0.749627,"pct_control":0.739204,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.999342,"donor_log2fc":0.0349123,"donor_lfcse":0.0731191,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":6.80131e-06},{"cell_type":"Oli","gene_symbol":"PSAP","gene_symbol_current":"PSAP","gene_id":"ENSG00000197746","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"lower in AD","nucleus_padj":0.234501,"nucleus_log2fc":-0.0153505,"nucleus_score":-1.29421,"pct_ad":0.659638,"pct_control":0.669467,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.984184,"donor_log2fc":0.00920469,"donor_lfcse":0.0694947,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.00235629}],"modules":[{"cell_type":"Ast","gene_id":"ENSG00000197746","module":"turquoise","kme":0.215477,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.796936,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"Ex","gene_id":"ENSG00000197746","module":"brown","kme":0.580495,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.552322,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"In","gene_id":"ENSG00000197746","module":"brown","kme":0.485637,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.852428,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"OPC","gene_id":"ENSG00000197746","module":"turquoise","kme":0.149501,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.680759,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"Oli","gene_id":"ENSG00000197746","module":"turquoise","kme":0.228795,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.842971,"module_significant":false}],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000197746","entrez_id":["5660"],"uniprot":["P07602"],"ncbi_summary":"This gene encodes a highly conserved preproprotein that is proteolytically processed to generate four main cleavage products including saposins A, B, C, and D. Each domain of the precursor protein is approximately 80 amino acid residues long with nearly identical placement of cysteine residues and glycosylation sites. Saposins A-D localize primarily to the lysosomal compartment where they facilitate the catabolism of glycosphingolipids with short oligosaccharide groups. The precursor protein exists both as a secretory protein and as an integral membrane protein and has neurotrophic activities. Mutations in this gene have been associated with Gaucher disease and metachromatic leukodystrophy. Alternative splicing results in multiple transcript variants, at least one of which encodes an isoform that is proteolytically processed. [provided by RefSeq, Feb 2016].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"P07602","text":"Saposins are specific low-molecular mass non-enzymatic glycoproteins that act as activator proteins for lysosomal sphingolipid-degrading enzymes, facilitating the hydrolysis of sphingolipids by extracting lipid substrates from membranes and presenting them to their respective enzymes. They are derived from a common precursor protein, prosaposin, which is proteolytically cleaved to yield four homologous proteins (saposin A, B, C, and D), each with distinct but partially overlapping lipid and enzyme specificities. [Prosaposin]: Behaves as a myelinotrophic and neurotrophic factor, these effects are mediated by its G protein-coupled receptors, GPR37 and GPR37L1, undergoing ligand-mediated internalization followed by ERK phosphorylation signaling. [Saposin-A]: Acts as an essential lysosomal cofactor that activates galactocerebrosidase (GALC, EC 3.2.1.46) by facilitating enzyme access to its membrane-embedded galactosylceramide substrate, the principal lipid component of myelin. [Saposin-B]: Stimulates the hydrolysis of galacto-cerebroside sulfate by arylsulfatase A ARSA (EC 3.1.6.8), GM1 gangliosides by beta-galactosidase (EC 3.2.1.23) and globotriaosylceramide by alpha-galactosidase A (EC 3.2.1.22). Forms a solubilizing complex with the substrates of the sphingolipid hydrolases. [Saposin-C]: Acts as an essential lysosomal cofactor that activates acid beta-glucosylceramidases (EC 3.2.1.45) by altering lipid bilayer properties to facilitate enzyme access to its membrane-embedded glucosylceramide substrate, while also protecting glucocerebrosidase from intralysosomal proteolytic degradation. [Saposin-D]: Acts as a lysosomal activator protein that stimulates acid ceramidase (EC 3.5.1.23) by solubilizing and presenting ceramide substrates at the lipid-water interface, facilitating their hydrolysis into sphingosine and fatty acids. It also activates acid sphingomyelinase (EC 3.1.4.12), though ceramide catabolism represents its primary physiological role."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_04","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}